3. Contextual Sentence-Aware Input Interpretation and Homophone Disambiguation
Resolving context-sensitive homophones (such as selecting ⁠暑い⁠ for ambient weather vs. ⁠熱い⁠ for hot objects when given ⁠あつい⁠) requires separating post-conversion linguistic tagging from neural or statistical sequence generation. Apple's native ⁠NaturalLanguage⁠ framework (⁠NLLanguageRecognizer⁠, ⁠NLTokenizer⁠, ⁠NLTagger⁠, ⁠NLEmbedding⁠) executes on-device across iOS 12+ with zero third-party binary footprint and supports ⁠.japanese⁠ and ⁠.korean⁠. Initializing ⁠NLTagger(tagSchemes: [.lexicalClass, .lemma, .nameType, .script])⁠ enables Swift native modules to extract stem lemmas and classify tokens as ⁠.noun⁠, ⁠.verb⁠, ⁠.adjective⁠, ⁠.particle⁠, ⁠.adverb⁠, or ⁠.classifier⁠. However, ⁠NaturalLanguage⁠ only analyzes already-orthographed text: it cannot generate Kanji candidates from Kana or output Japanese Katakana readings. Consequently, ⁠NLTagger⁠ should serve as LingoMingle's fast iOS answer validator—verifying particle choice, lemmatizing inflected learner responses before ⁠JMdict⁠/⁠KRDict⁠ lookup, and confirming expected parts of speech—rather than an input conversion engine. [1][2][3][4][5][6]
Shipping a companion system-wide iOS custom keyboard (⁠UIInputViewController⁠) introduces severe OS constraints documented in iOS extension engineering guides. Apple enforces a strict per-process Jetsam memory limit where exceeding the ceiling causes silent termination—with no crash log, signal, or exception—abruptly reverting the user to the system keyboard. Historical developer reports placed extension limits at ~30–40 MB (iOS 8–13), while recent production debugging places the practical ceiling near ~60 MB of ⁠phys_footprint⁠ (an anecdotal Reddit developer comment claims Apple raised limits past 120 MB on iPhone 15 and newer hardware, though Apple's official documentation publishes no fixed MB guarantee). Furthermore, iOS allocates a new ⁠UIInputViewController⁠ per host app switch; retained view hierarchies or Swift closure cycles stack allocations across launches past 70 MB. Production mitigations require avoiding ⁠loadView⁠ overrides when embedding C++ IME cores, implementing ⁠didReceiveMemoryWarning()⁠, and tearing down views inside ⁠deinit⁠ deferred by one main-queue turn rather than inside ⁠viewWillDisappear⁠ (which forces costly rebuilds during temporary hides). Functionally, ⁠UIInputViewController⁠ extensions lack access to Apple's system autocorrect, predictive text, and host-app text selection (precluding inline highlight-and-convert behavior inside third-party apps), disable automatically inside ⁠secureTextEntry⁠ or phone-pad fields, and require ⁠RequestsOpenAccess = YES⁠ in ⁠Info.plist⁠ for key-click sound and read-only App Group access—plus explicit user "Allow Full Access" consent in Settings for network calls or App Group writes. Extensions must therefore inspect preceding text via ⁠textDocumentProxy.documentContextBeforeInput⁠, optionally load user shortcuts via ⁠UILexicon⁠ (⁠requestSupplementaryLexiconWithCompletion⁠), and render candidate strips internally. [1][2][3][4][5][6]
These constraints mandate a two-tier hybrid conversion architecture in LingoMingle's Swift bridge. Inside any ⁠UIInputViewController⁠ keyboard extension, neural language models exceed safe memory budgets on baseline iOS devices; the extension must compile either ⁠kishikawakatsumi/mozc-for-ios⁠ (Apache 2.0 port of Mozc) or ⁠azooKey/AzooKeyKanaKanjiConverter⁠ in its non-Zenzai configuration without the GGML backend. (Note: while Mozc is widely known for statistical IME conversion, exact binary size, RAM usage, and internal LOUDS/Viterbi implementation benchmarks for ⁠mozc-for-ios⁠ were absent from available repository documentation.) Inside LingoMingle's main app process—free of keyboard-extension Jetsam caps—a Swift Expo Module can pair ⁠AzooKeyKanaKanjiConverter⁠ with the neural Zenzai backend (⁠zenz-v1⁠ or ⁠zenz-v2.5⁠) for sentence-aware Kana-Kanji and homophone disambiguation. Released in January 2025 and trained on the 129M-row ⁠Miwa-Keita/zenz-v2.5-dataset⁠, ⁠zenz-v2.5⁠ adapts Japanese GPT-2 models (⁠ku-nlp/gpt2-small-japanese-char⁠ / ⁠gpt2-medium-japanese-char⁠) with a character-level + byte-level BPE tokenizer and ⁠llama.cpp⁠ GGUF quantization (⁠Q8_0⁠) across three tiers: ⁠zenz-v2.5-xsmall⁠ (22.5M / 26M parameters), ⁠zenz-v2.5-small⁠ (90.5M / 91M parameters), and ⁠zenz-v2.5-medium⁠ (0.3B / 310M parameters), alongside alternative GGUF checkpoint ⁠togatogah/jinen-v1-small⁠. Downstream implementers must verify the Swift package license of ⁠AzooKeyKanaKanjiConverter⁠ directly in its repository (unspecified in retrieved index pages), distribute ⁠zenz-v2.5⁠ weights as attributable CC-BY-SA 4.0 downloadable packs rather than closed modified binaries, and profile quantized GGUF RAM and ms/token latency on target iOS and Android devices—since neither mobile inference benchmarks nor an Android ⁠AzooKey⁠ equivalent appeared in available sources (Android contextual conversion requires either a custom ⁠llama.cpp⁠ JNI wrapper running ⁠zenz-v2.5⁠ GGUF or a server endpoint). 