4. Second Language Acquisition Science and Interactive Exercise Specifications
LingoMingle's curriculum engine is structured around Paul Nation's Four Strands framework (2007), which allocates study time roughly equally across four distinct learning conditions. Meaning-Focused Input (~25%) requires extensive reading and listening on familiar topics where 95–98% of running words are already known (Laufer 1992 places adequate comprehension around 95%; Schmitt 2011 places unassisted reading around 98%), aligning with Krashen's i + 1 principle so learners process meaning rather than constantly decoding. Meaning-Focused Output (~25%) operationalizes Swain's (1995) Output Hypothesis: speaking and writing on familiar topics forces learners to notice structural gaps, and research summaries cite production as more effective than passive comprehension for acquiring vocabulary and grammar. Language-Focused Learning (capped at ≤25%) covers deliberate "focus on formS" instruction on pronunciation, orthography, vocabulary, grammar, and dictation; Laufer (2006) observed superior Phase-1 vocabulary gains from focus-on-formS (though that advantage disappeared after Phase 2), and Nation cautions that exceeding 25% starves fluency and communicative use. Fluency Development (~25%) strictly bars unknown vocabulary or difficult grammar (requiring 100% known items) and applies speed pressure—exemplified by Nation's 4-3-2 speaking/retelling task (repeating the same talk across shrinking timers, e.g., 4, 3, and 2 minutes, or 3/2/1 minutes in classroom adaptations) and timed rereading—to automatize retrieval. [1][2][3]
Task depth within each exercise is quantified via Laufer and Hulstijn's Involvement Load Hypothesis (ILH) (2001), built on Craik and Lockhart's (1972) Depth of Processing model. ILH scores every task across three factors—Need (motivational: 0 = none, 1 = externally assigned, 2 = learner-initiated), Search (cognitive: 0 = gloss supplied, 1 = learner looks up unknown meaning; some frameworks score form lookup at 2), and Evaluation (cognitive: 0 = isolated exposure, 1 = comparing candidate words in a provided context such as fill-in-the-blank, 2 = comparing and deploying words in a self-created original context)—generating 15 theoretical combinations. Empirical support includes Hulstijn and Laufer's (2001) multi-country university study (which partially supported ILH, though secondary literature notes methodological debates) and a primary-school study of 28 ten-year-old EFL learners across four conditions where higher involvement load (Need 1 + Search 1 + Evaluation 2 = 4) produced statistically significant gains on both receptive and productive tests (p = .000 < .05). Crucially, a systematic review (⁠PMC9598591⁠) found that 30.77% of ILH studies tested only one aspect of word knowledge, 33.33% tested two, 26.92% tested three, 6.41% tested four, and zero tested five or all six aspects—meaning LingoMingle must explicitly pair receptive recognition probes with productive recall and contextual composition. [1][2][3]
For spaced-repetition scheduling inside Language-Focused Learning, LingoMingle can deploy the open-source TypeScript FSRS engine ⁠open-spaced-repetition/ts-fsrs⁠ (which tracks three numerical memory-state variables per flashcard, with ⁠femto-fsrs⁠ available as a minimal alternative; exact underlying equations and parameter names were not present in the retrieved package summaries). Combining Dual Coding, Nation's Four Strands, ILH, and ⁠ts-fsrs⁠ produces seven concrete mobile exercise formats: [1][2][3]
